  abstract = {High-temperature oxide melt solution calorimetry and Rietveld refinements of powder X-ray diffraction data were used to investigate the structure (Fd3m; Z=8) and energetics of a series of RE2Ti2O7 (RE=Sm--Lu) compounds with the pyrochlore structure as well as La2Ti2O7 with a layered perovskite-type structure. All of the RE-titanates were found to be stable in enthalpy with respect to their oxides. In the pyrochlore series, Lu2Ti2O7 was least stable in enthalpy ({$\Delta$}Hf-ox at 298K=-56.0\textpm 4.0kJ/mol); the most stable materials were Gd-, Eu-, and Sm2Ti2O7 with {$\Delta$}Hf-ox at 298K=-113.4\textpm 2.7, -106.1\textpm 4.2, -115.4\textpm 4.2kJ/mol, respectively. In general, as the radius ratio of the A- to B-site cations, RA/RB, decreases, the pyrochlore structure becomes less stable. The trend of ionic radius of the RE3+ cation vs. {$\Delta$}Hf-ox at 298K is non-linear and approximately parallels the increasing ``resistance'' to ion-beam-induced amorphization as RA/RB decreases.},
